  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/1477B3">Remark <span data-tag="1477B3">6.2.3.9</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be a presentable $\infty $-category in which colimits are universal. Then any monomorphism $u: U \rightarrow \coprod X_{\alpha }$ can be obtained as a coproduct of maps $u_{\alpha }: U_{\alpha } \rightarrow X_{\alpha }$, where each $u_{\alpha }$ is a pullback of $u$ and therefore also a monomorphism. It follows that the natural map </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \theta : \Sub ( \coprod X_{\alpha } ) \rightarrow \prod \Sub (X_{\alpha }) \]
</div>
  <p> is a monomorphism of partially ordered sets. If coproducts in $\calX $ are disjoint, then $\theta $ is bijective. </p>
</article>